-- ============================================================================
--  0097 — the rest of the maths bank reads College Board's own text
--
--  591 Mathematics questions were still a picture of the whole page (0085),
--  with a stem that only said so, options left blank or in pieces, and an
--  explanation read off the page with its mathematics missing ("when x is  or
--  2, ."). They now read as the rest of the bank does: the question as text,
--  its options as text under their labels, the explanation whole, and a
--  picture of the chart, table or drawn figure alone where there is one.
--
--  The text is not read off the page. It is College Board's own, from the
--  educator question bank the questions came from: the MathML of the current
--  items, and for the older ones the words College Board gives a screen reader
--  for each piece of mathematics, read back into the bank's notation
--  (tools/questionbank/collegeboard.py, spoken.py). Where those words were
--  wrong or missing, the expression was read off College Board's image by eye
--  (spoken_fixes.json). Every multiple-choice key and every accepted answer
--  was checked against the bank and agreed, so neither is written here.
--
--  Figures are drawn from College Board's own HTML (render_figures.mjs): 174
--  questions keep one, and 13 have four graphs or tables for options (0095).
--  The other 417 had nothing to show beyond the mathematics now in the text,
--  so their picture goes.
--
--  APPLY AFTER THE APP IS DEPLOYED with these figures in
--  apps/web/public/question-figures/cb, never before: until then the pictures
--  these rows point at do not exist.
--
--  Each update is by source_ref and only of a question whose stem is still the
--  one it had when this was written, so a question corrected by hand since is
--  not touched, and a second run changes nothing. A question's options and
--  key are written before its stem, while that check still holds.
-- ============================================================================

update question_options o set body = $qb$x = 46$qb$, image_url = null from questions q where q.id = o.question_id and o.label = 'A' and q.source_ref = 'CB-0013adbd' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '5ab38a70';
update question_options o set body = $qb$y = 46$qb$, image_url = null from questions q where q.id = o.question_id and o.label = 'B' and q.source_ref = 'CB-0013adbd' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '5ab38a70';
update question_options o set body = $qb$w = 134$qb$, image_url = null from questions q where q.id = o.question_id and o.label = 'C' and q.source_ref = 'CB-0013adbd' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '5ab38a70';
update question_options o set body = $qb$z = 134$qb$, image_url = null from questions q where q.id = o.question_id and o.label = 'D' and q.source_ref = 'CB-0013adbd' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '5ab38a70';
update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$Choice A is correct. In the figure shown, line t, which is a transversal, intersects lines j and k. If two lines are intersected by a transversal and the measures of a pair of same-side interior angles sum to 180°, then the lines are parallel. In the figure, the angle measuring 134° and the angle measuring x° are a pair of same-side interior angles. If x = 46, (134 + x)° = (134 + 46)°, or (134 + x)° = 180°. Therefore, x = 46 is sufficient to prove that lines j and k are parallel.
Choice B is incorrect. The angle measuring y° and the angle measuring 134° are alternate interior angles. If y = 46 rather than y = 134, then lines j and k would not be parallel.
Choice C is incorrect. The angle measuring w° and the angle measuring 134° are vertical angles. Thus, w = 134 whether lines j and k are parallel or not.
Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.$qb$ from questions q where q.id = k.question_id and q.source_ref = 'CB-0013adbd' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '5ab38a70';
