update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$Choice C is correct. It is assumed that the oil and gas production decreased at a constant rate. Therefore, the function f that best models the production t years after the year 2000 can be written as a linear function, f(t) = mt + b, where m is the rate of change of the oil and gas production and b is the oil and gas production, in millions of barrels, in the year 2000. Since there were 4 million barrels of oil and gas produced in 2000, b = 4. The rate of change, m, can be calculated as (4 - 1.9)/(0 - 13) = -2.1/13, which is equivalent to -21/130, the rate of change in choice C.
