update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$Choice C is correct. To express the sum of the two rational expressions on the left-hand side of the equation as the single rational expression on the right-hand side of the equation, the expressions on the left-hand side must have the same denominator. Multiplying the first expression by (x + 5)/(x - 5) results in (2(x + 5))/((x - 2)(x + 5)), and multiplying the second expression by (x - 2)/(x - 2) results in (3(x - 2))/((x - 2)(x + 5)), so the given equation can be rewritten as (2(x + 5))/((x - 2)(x + 5)) + (3(x - 2))/((x - 2)(x + 5)) = (rx + t)/((x - 2)(x + 5)), or (2x + 10)/((x - 2)(x + 5)) + (3x - 6)/((x - 2)(x + 5)) = (rx + t)/((x - 2)(x + 5)). Since the two rational expressions on the left-hand side of the equation have the same denominator as the rational expression on the right-hand side of the equation, it follows that (2x + 10) + (3x - 6) = rx + t. Combining like terms on the left-hand side yields 5x + 4 = rx + t, so it follows that r = 5 and t = 4. Therefore, the value of rt is 5(4) = 20.
Choice A is incorrect and may result from an error when determining the sign of either r or t. Choice B is incorrect and may result from not distributing the 2 and 3 to their respective terms in (2(x + 5))/((x - 2)(x + 5)) + (3(x - 2))/((x - 2)(x + 5)) = (rx + t)/((x - 2)(x + 5)). Choice D is incorrect and may result from a calculation error.$qb$ from questions q where q.id = k.question_id and q.source_ref = 'CB-34847f8a' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = 'c7d347d4';
