update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$Choice A is correct. It is given that line l is perpendicular to a line whose equation is x = 2. A line whose equation is a constant value of x is vertical, so l must therefore be horizontal. Horizontal lines have a slope of 0, so l has a slope of 0.
Choice B is incorrect. A line with slope -1/2 is perpendicular to a line with slope 2. However, the line with equation x = 2 is vertical and has undefined slope (not slope of 2). Choice C is incorrect. A line with slope –2 is perpendicular to a line with slope 1/2. However, the line with equation x = 2 has undefined slope (not slope of 1/2). Choice D is incorrect; this is the slope of the line x = 2 itself, not the slope of a line perpendicular to it.$qb$ from questions q where q.id = k.question_id and q.source_ref = 'CB-98d3393a' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '91e891de';
update questions set stem = $qb$Line l in the xy-plane is perpendicular to the line with equation x = 2. What is the slope of line l?$qb$, image_url = null, updated_at = now() where source_ref = 'CB-98d3393a' and left(md5(stem), 8) = '91e891de';
update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$The correct answer is 12/5. It's given that in right triangle XYZ, ∠X and ∠Z are acute angles. It follows that ∠Y is a right angle. It's given that point R lies on X̅Z̅ such that Y̅R̅ is perpendicular to X̅Z̅. Since ∠Y is a right angle and is comprised of ∠XYR and ∠ZYR, it follows that the sum of the measures of ∠XYR and ∠ZYR is 90°. It's given that the measure of ∠XYR is b°. It follows that the measure of ∠ZYR is (90 - b)°. Since Y̅R̅ is perpendicular to X̅Z̅, it follows that ∠XRY and ∠ZRY are right angles. It follows that ∆XRY and ∆ZRY are right triangles. It's given that the measure of ∠XZY is a°. Because the sum of the measures of the interior angles of a triangle is 180°, it follows that the measures of ∠ZRY, ∠XZY, and ∠ZYR have a sum of 180°. Since the measure of ∠ZRY is 90°, the measure of ∠YZR is a°, and the measure of ∠ZYR is (90 - b)°, it follows that 90 + a + 90 - b = 180, or a - b + 180 = 180. Subtracting 180 from both sides of this equation yields a - b = 0. Adding b to both sides of this equation yields a = b. It's given that sin a° = 12/13. Since a = b, it follows that sin b° = 12/13. In a right triangle, the sine of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the hypotenuse. It follows that, for a positive constant k, 12k represents the length of the leg opposite ∠XYR and 13k represents the length of the hypotenuse of △XRY. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. Let nk represent the length of the leg adjacent to ∠XYR. It follows that (13k)^(2) = (12k)^(2) + (nk)^(2), or 169k^(2) = 144k^(2) + n^(2)k^(2). Subtracting 144k^(2) from both sides of this equation yields 25k^(2) = n^(2)k^(2). Taking the positive square root of both sides of this equation yields 5k = nk. Therefore, the length of the leg adjacent to ∠XYR is 5k. In a right triangle, the tangent of an acute angle is the ratio of the length of the leg opposite the angle to the length of the leg adjacent to the angle. It follows that tan b° = 12k/5k, or tan b° = 12/5. Note that 12/5 and 2.4 are examples of ways to enter a correct answer.$qb$ from questions q where q.id = k.question_id and q.source_ref = 'CB-98e02472' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '2bd97ceb';
