update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$Choice B is correct. A model for a quantity C that decreases by a certain percentage per time period t is an exponential equation in the form C = I(1 - r/100)^(t), where I is the initial value at time t = 0 for r% annual decline. It’s given that C is the number of millions of CDs sold in the United States and that t is the number of years after 2005. It’s also given that 600 million CDs were sold at time t = 0, so I = 600. This number declines by 15% per year, so r = 15. Substituting these values into the equation produces C = 600(1 - 15/100)^(t), or C = 600(0.85)^(t).
Choice A is incorrect and may result from errors made when representing the percent decline. Choices C and D are incorrect. These equations model exponential increases in CD sales, not exponential decreases.$qb$ from questions q where q.id = k.question_id and q.source_ref = 'CB-a31417d1' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = '1436743c';
