If (x, y) is a solution to the system of equations above, which of the following could be the value of x?$qb$, image_url = null, updated_at = now() where source_ref = 'CB-d0a7871e' and left(md5(stem), 8) = 'b34c7e5e';
update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$The correct answer is 76. It's given that the graph of y = g(x) is the result of translating the graph of y = f(x) up 4 units in the xy-plane. It follows that the graph of y = g(x) is the same as the graph of y = f(x) + 4. Substituting g(x) for y in the equation y = f(x) + 4 yields g(x) = f(x) + 4. It's given that f(x) = (x - 6)(x - 2)(x + 6). Substituting (x - 6)(x - 2)(x + 6) for f(x) in the equation g(x) = f(x) + 4 yields g(x) = (x - 6)(x - 2)(x + 6) + 4. Substituting 0 for x in this equation yields g(0) = (0 - 6)(0 - 2)(0 + 6) + 4, or g(0) = 76. Thus, the value of g(0) is 76.$qb$ from questions q where q.id = k.question_id and q.source_ref = 'CB-d135f4bf' and left(md5(q.stem), 8) = 'b80eaf32';
update questions set stem = $qb$The function f is defined by f(x) = (x - 6)(x - 2)(x + 6). In the xy-plane, the graph of y = g(x) is the result of translating the graph of y = f(x) up 4 units. What is the value of g(0)?$qb$, image_url = null, updated_at = now() where source_ref = 'CB-d135f4bf' and left(md5(stem), 8) = 'b80eaf32';
