update question_keys k set explanation = $qb$Choice C is correct. Factoring –1 from the second, third, and fourth terms gives x^(2)– c^(2)– 2cd – d^(2)= x^(2)– (c^(2)+ 2cd + d^(2)). The expression c^(2) + 2cd + d^(2)is the expanded form of a perfect square: c^(2) + 2cd + d^(2) = (c + d)^(2). Therefore, x^(2)– (c^(2)+ 2cd + d^(2)) = x^(2) – (c + d)^(2). Since a = c + d, x^(2)– (c + d)^(2)= x^(2)– a^(2). Finally, because x^(2)– a^(2) is the difference of squares, it can be expanded as x^(2)– a^(2)= (x + a)(x – a).
